-- title:
--   Kernel of (Q,R)-evaluation is generated by A-1
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a field, let $N$ be a natural number with $0 < N$, and let $\zeta \in K$ be a primitive $N$-th root of unity. Let $Q, R \in K[[q]]$ be power series whose constant coefficients both equal $1$ and which satisfy $Q^3 \neq R^2$. Let $A \in K[X_0, X_1]$ (polynomials in two variables indexed by `Fin 2`) be weighted homogeneous of degree $N$ for the weights $4$ on $X_0$ and $6$ on $X_1$, squarefree, and such that the $K$-algebra evaluation of $A$ at $(Q, R)$ equals $1$ in $K[[q]]$. Then the kernel of the evaluation ring homomorphism $K[X_0, X_1] \to K[[q]]$ sending $X_0 \mapsto Q$ and $X_1 \mapsto R$ is the principal ideal generated by $A - 1$.
--
--   This is the abstract form of part (iv) of Swinnerton-Dyer's theorem on the structure of the algebra generated by the reductions of the Eisenstein series $Q = E_4$ and $R = E_6$; in the intended application $K = \mathbb{F}_\ell$, $N = \ell - 1$ and $A$ is the reduction of $E_{\ell-1}$. It is used to show that the weight of a level-one modular form whose $q$-expansion is congruent to a constant is divisible by the relevant index, via [`ModularForm.sub_one_dvd_weight_of_qExpansion_congr_const_levelOne`](thm.html#ModularForm.sub_one_dvd_weight_of_qExpansion_congr_const_levelOne).
-- source:
